      "traceTextDiagram": "Frontend App Bootstrap (packages/gui/src/)\n├── index.html \u003c-- packages/gui/index.html:1\n│   └── \u003cdiv id=\"root\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e \u003c-- packages/gui/index.html:10\n│       └── \u003cscript src=\"/src/main.tsx\"\u003e \u003c-- packages/gui/index.html:11\n│           └── main.tsx\n│               ├── QueryClient creation \u003c-- 1b\n│               ├── Theme initialization \u003c-- packages/gui/src/main.tsx:18\n│               └── createRoot().render() \u003c-- 1a\n│                   └── \u003cQueryClientProvider\u003e \u003c-- 1c\n│                       └── \u003cApp\u003e \u003c-- packages/gui/src/main.tsx:31\n│                           └── App.tsx\n│                               └── \u003cBrowserRouter\u003e \u003c-- 1d\n│                                   └── \u003cRoutes\u003e \u003c-- packages/gui/src/App.tsx:22\n│                                       └── \u003cRoute path=\"/\"\u003e \u003c-- packages/gui/src/App.tsx:23\n│                                           └── \u003cLayout\u003e\n│                                               └── Layout.tsx\n│                                                   ├── useSSE() \u003c-- 1e\n│                                                   ├── \u003cSidebar\u003e \u003c-- packages/gui/src/components/layout/Layout.tsx:12\n│                                                   ├── \u003cHeader\u003e \u003c-- packages/gui/src/components/layout/Layout.tsx:14\n│                                                   └── \u003cOutlet /\u003e \u003c-- 1f",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nThe foxctl GUI is a **React-based dashboard** that provides a visual interface for monitoring and managing foxctl operations. Without it, users would need to interact with foxctl entirely through CLI commands or direct API calls. The GUI solves this by offering **real-time job monitoring, interactive console sessions, and data exploration** through a modern web interface.\n\nThe system follows a **client-server architecture**: a React SPA (Single Page Application) runs in the browser and communicates with a Go backend server that provides REST APIs, Server-Sent Events for live updates, and WebSocket connections for interactive features.\n\n## Details\n\n### Application Bootstrap\n\nThe entry point is **`index.html`** which loads the React application via `main.tsx` [1a]. The bootstrap sequence initializes three critical pieces:\n\n1. **TanStack Query Client** [1b] - Manages server state, caching, and automatic refetching of API data\n2. **Theme System** - Loads dark/light mode preference from localStorage before first render to prevent flash\n3. **React Router** [1d] - Enables client-side navigation between pages (jobs, tasks, console, etc.)\n\nThe `createRoot().render()` call [1a] wraps the entire app in a `QueryClientProvider` [1c], making the query client available to all components via hooks.\n\n### Layout and Real-time Updates\n\nThe **Layout component** serves as the shell for all pages, providing the sidebar navigation and header. Critically, it initializes the **SSE (Server-Sent Events) connection** [1e] immediately on mount, establishing a persistent connection to `/api/events` for receiving real-time invalidation events from the backend.\n\nThe `\u003cOutlet /\u003e` component [1f] acts as a placeholder where React Router injects the current route's page component (e.g., JobsPage, ConsolePage). This means every page automatically benefits from the SSE connection without needing to set it up individually.\n\n### Development vs Production\n\nDuring development, **Vite's dev server** runs on port 5173 and proxies `/api/*` and `/ws/*` requests to the Go backend on port 8090. This enables hot module replacement while maintaining full API connectivity.\n\nIn production, the Go server can optionally serve the **built static files** from `packages/gui/dist/`, eliminating the need for a separate frontend server. The server checks request paths and prioritizes API/WebSocket routes over static file serving to prevent conflicts."
    },
