        },
        {
          "id": "7e",
          "path": "internal/interfaces/web/server.go",
          "lineNumber": 134,
          "lineContent": "if strings.HasPrefix(r.URL.Path, \"/api/\") || strings.HasPrefix(r.URL.Path, \"/ws/\") {",
          "title": "Route Precedence",
          "description": "Handler checks for API/WS prefixes first, preventing static files from shadowing endpoints"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Production Build \u0026 Deployment Flow\n├── Frontend Build Process\n│   └── npm run build \u003c-- 7a\n│       └── Outputs to packages/gui/dist/\n├── CLI Command Setup\n│   └── foxctl web serve \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/web.go:25\n│       └── --ui-dir flag definition \u003c-- 7b\n│           └── Accepts path to built UI files\n├── Server Initialization\n│   └── web.NewServer(opts) \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/web.go:84\n│       └── opts.UIDir set from flag \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/web.go:81\n│           └── UIDir != \"\" check \u003c-- 7c\n│               ├── http.FileServer() \u003c-- 7d\n│               │   └── Serves static files\n│               └── Route handler logic \u003c-- internal/interfaces/web/server.go:132\n│                   └── API/WS prefix check \u003c-- 7e\n│                       ├── /api/* → 404 \u003c-- internal/interfaces/web/server.go:135\n│                       ├── /ws/* → 404 (not static)\n│                       └── /* → serve \u003c-- internal/interfaces/web/server.go:138\n└── Result: Single binary serves both\n    API + SPA",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nThe foxctl GUI is a **React single-page application** that needs to be deployed alongside a **Go backend server**. In development, developers want hot-reload for frontend changes, but in production, they want a **single binary** that serves both the API and the UI. This trace shows how the system supports both modes: development uses Vite's dev server with proxying [6b, 6c], while production bundles the React app into static files that the Go server can optionally serve [7a, 7d].\n\n## Details\n\n### Build Process\n\nThe frontend build is triggered via `npm run build` [7a], which runs TypeScript compilation followed by Vite's production bundler. This outputs optimized static files (HTML, JS, CSS) to `packages/gui/dist/`.\n\n### Server Configuration\n\nThe `foxctl web serve` command accepts a `--ui-dir` flag [7b] that specifies where built UI files are located. When this flag is provided, the server enables static file serving [7c].\n\n### Route Handling\n\nThe Go server creates an `http.FileServer` [7d] to serve files from the specified directory. **Critically**, the route handler checks incoming requests first [7e]: if the path starts with `/api/` or `/ws/`, it returns 404 (preventing static files from shadowing API endpoints). Otherwise, it serves the static file, allowing the React router to handle client-side navigation.\n\n### Development vs Production\n\nIn **development**, the `--ui-dir` flag is omitted, so the server only serves API endpoints. Developers run Vite separately on port 5173, which proxies `/api` and `/ws` requests to the Go server on port 8090 [6b, 6c].\n\nIn **production**, operators build the frontend once, then start the server with `--ui-dir ./packages/gui/dist`, creating a single process that handles everything."
    }
  ],
  "description": "Maps the complete flow from React SPA initialization through API/WebSocket communication to Go backend handlers. Frontend bootstraps at [1a], fetches data via [2b]→[2d], receives real-time updates via [3c]→[3e], and maintains console sessions via [4b]→[4f]. Backend starts at [5a], registers routes at [5d], handles API requests at [6c], streams SSE events at [7d], and manages WebSocket sessions at [8c].",
